  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Trevor McDonald couldn't hold the line. Three and two-thirds innings, four runs, and the game fell apart before it could even start.",
    "what_this_means": "This is what inconsistency looks like in real time. McDonald and the relief corps that followed actually held. Sanmartin's two runs were the only damage after the starter left, and the last four relievers combined for five clean innings. That's not the problem. The problem is nine runners left in scoring position and zero conversions. They had chances in the second, the fourth, the fifth. Arraez drove in the one run that mattered, but the offense had already surrendered before the third inning ended. When a team doesn't score until someone else does it for them, the math stops working. They never led. They never had a moment where the outcome was in doubt. That's the difference between a loss and a statement about who they are right now. And this one was both.",
    "what_to_watch": "Chicago's offense was fine. McDonald just wasn't. And when your starter leaves after the fourth inning with a 4\u20131 hole, the bullpen's excellence becomes irrelevant. This is what costs teams wins in June: not the pitching that follows, but the pitching that starts, and the bats that stay asleep when they have the chance to wake up. Nine left on base is a choice. They chose to accept it.",
